{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"COMPLETION-VERIFIER REMEDIATION — the one unmet criterion is now implemented, not argued around.\n\nTHE FINDING WAS CORRECT. The AC has two halves: (a) `docs/user-guide.md` documents selecting the DC\nbackend and installing the extra, and (b) THE DOCUMENTED COMMANDS HAVE BEEN EXECUTED AGAINST THE J5\nHARNESS. Half (a) was satisfied (user-guide.md:325-371). Half (b) was NOT, and the verifier's reason\nfor saying so was exact: the `jira-dc-harness` CI job runs the smoke tests, and none of them executes\nthe commands the guide actually tells an operator to run. Nothing in the repo demonstrated the\ndocumented path had ever been driven.\n\nI did not re-word the criterion. Executing the documented commands is a day's-worth-of-operator-time\nquestion — a setup guide is precisely the artifact where an untested instruction is expensive, because\nthe reader follows it literally, it fails, and they cannot tell whether the guide or their instance is\nwrong.\n\nWHAT LANDED — change 1115, `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_user_guide_commands.py`.\n\nThe config block is PARSED OUT of `docs/user-guide.md` at runtime rather than restated in the test.\nThat distinction is the whole value: a hand-copied duplicate of the documented TOML would keep passing\nwhile the guide rotted around it, which is the exact failure this criterion exists to prevent. If\nsomeone edits the documented block into something that no longer selects the DC backend, or renames a\nconfig key, the test fails.\n\nA CONFIG-SURFACE POINT I CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. rebar reads bare `[reconciler]` from a\nstandalone `rebar.toml` and `[tool.rebar.reconciler]` from a `pyproject.toml`\n(`_config_sources.py:111-115`). The guide documents the `[tool.rebar.…]` spelling, and the existing\nlive module (`test_reconcile_pass.py`) writes the OTHER form into `rebar.toml`. So driving the guide\nthrough that module's fixture would have proven the guide works by not using it. The new test writes a\n`pyproject.toml` — the surface on which the documented spelling is the correct one. Verified the\nsubstituted block parses and resolves to\n`{'backend': 'jira-datacenter', 'base_url': ..., 'allow_insecure': True}` with\n`project` under `[tool.rebar.jira]`.\n\nThree substitutions are made into the documented block, and each is itself documented in the same\nsection, so this stays a test OF the guide: `base_url` (the guide's value is an illustrative\nexample.gov URL) -> the harness; `project` -> the scratch project; and `allow_insecure = true`, which\nthe guide documents as the supported answer for a non-https instance and explicitly scopes to \"a\nloopback test instance, not a production bridge\". The harness is plain http, so following the guide\nFOR THIS DEPLOYMENT means setting it.\n\nWHAT IS ASSERTED, BEYOND \"EXIT 0\"\n * the dry-run emits a `no_write` envelope. A run that exited 0 without one did not complete a pass —\n   and a guide that tells operators to \"inspect before enabling live sync\" and then WRITES would be\n   actively dangerous, so the no-write property is asserted rather than assumed from the flag name.\n * `mutation_failures == 0`.\n * no traceback on stderr.\n * the documented extra REALLY EXISTS: the `pip install 'nava-rebar[jira-datacenter]'` command is\n   matched out of the guide and checked against `pyproject.toml`'s distribution name and\n   `[project.optional-dependencies]`. Deliberately harness-FREE — a typo'd extra name is a\n   documentation defect discoverable from the repo alone, and making that check wait on a live\n   instance would leave a cheap verification unmade. Confirmed today: `nava-rebar` matches\n   `project.name`, and `jira-datacenter` is declared at pyproject.toml:168.\n * JIRA_PAT is ENV-ONLY. The guide presents this as a security property (\"never a config key, so the\n   credential cannot be checked into a repo by accident\"). A documented security guarantee that\n   nothing checks is the weakest kind of claim, so the test writes the PAT INTO the config file,\n   removes it from the environment, and requires the pass to FAIL with an error naming the variable —\n   proving both that the config value is not honoured and that there is no anonymous fallback (which\n   would turn a missing credential into a silently empty pass).\n\nSTATUS OF THE EVIDENCE. Change 1115 is pushed and awaiting `LLM-Review +1` / `Verified +1`. The\ncriterion is satisfied when the `jira DC harness smoke` job runs this module green — that job is the\nonly place the documented commands CAN be executed, since the harness base image is linux/amd64 only\nand this workstation is Apple Silicon (`tests/external/live_jira_dc/README.md:54-73`). I will record\nthe job result here before closing, and I will read the JOB, not the overall run status."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785521453283439001,"uuid":"980bb2c4-ce30-4406-a146-ec88bb7d3ec9"}